			<item>
				<title>Integers</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/integers/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/integers/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;integers&#34;&gt;Integers&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#integers&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Go doesn&amp;rsquo;t have &lt;em&gt;an&lt;/em&gt; integer type. It has a whole menu — and the compiler&#xA;makes you order precisely. This exercise&amp;rsquo;s stub looks innocent: multiply and&#xA;add. It doesn&amp;rsquo;t compile as written, and the reason is the entire lesson.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-menu&#34;&gt;The menu&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#the-menu&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;uint&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// platform-sized: 32 bits on 32-bit systems, 64 on 64-bit&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int8&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int16&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int32&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int64&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;uint8&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;uint16&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;uint32&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;uint64&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Signed or unsigned, then a width. &lt;code&gt;uint8&lt;/code&gt; holds 0–255 (you&amp;rsquo;ve met its&#xA;wrapping temper in the overflow exercise); &lt;code&gt;int64&lt;/code&gt; stretches past nine&#xA;quintillion in both directions. &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;uint&lt;/code&gt; follow the platform —&#xA;write &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt; for everyday counting and only reach for a width when the&#xA;protocol, the file format, or memory demands it.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Variables</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/variables/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/variables/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;variables&#34;&gt;Variables&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#variables&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Every value lives somewhere. Go gives you two ways to say &amp;ldquo;a box called&#xA;&lt;code&gt;x&lt;/code&gt;&amp;rdquo; — and the difference between them is the difference between Go&amp;rsquo;s two&#xA;audiences: the compiler that must know everything, and the human who&amp;rsquo;d&#xA;rather not repeat it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-two-declarations&#34;&gt;The two declarations&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#the-two-declarations&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; = &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;21&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// long form: name, type, value — everything explicit&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;x&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// short form: name and value; the type is inferred&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;var x int = 21&lt;/code&gt; states the type out loud. &lt;code&gt;y := x * 2&lt;/code&gt; lets the compiler&#xA;infer it — &lt;code&gt;y&lt;/code&gt; is an &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt; because &lt;code&gt;x * 2&lt;/code&gt; is one. Same result, less ink.&#xA;The short form exists because most of the time the right-hand side already&#xA;shouts the type; writing it twice helps nobody.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>If Else</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/if_else/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/if_else/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;if-else&#34;&gt;If Else&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#if-else&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;After the ceremony of types and declarations, a relief: Go&amp;rsquo;s &lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt; works the&#xA;way you&amp;rsquo;d guess. Almost. The differences from the C family are small,&#xA;deliberate, and each one removes a historical bug class.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-shape&#34;&gt;The shape&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#the-shape&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Syntax: if &amp;lt;condition&amp;gt; { &amp;lt;then&amp;gt; } else { &amp;lt;otherwise&amp;gt; }&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Max&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    } &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Two things to notice. First, &lt;strong&gt;no parentheses&lt;/strong&gt; around the condition —&#xA;&lt;code&gt;if (a &amp;gt;= b)&lt;/code&gt; doesn&amp;rsquo;t compile. The braces do the grouping; parentheses&#xA;would be noise. Second, &lt;strong&gt;braces are mandatory&lt;/strong&gt;, even for one-line bodies.&#xA;Dangling-else ambiguity and the &amp;ldquo;added a second line outside the &lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt;&amp;rdquo;&#xA;disaster simply can&amp;rsquo;t be written.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Panics</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/panics/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/panics/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;panics&#34;&gt;Panics&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#panics&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Some errors shouldn&amp;rsquo;t be handled. They should stop everything, loudly, with&#xA;a stack trace pointing at the crime scene. Go&amp;rsquo;s word for that is &lt;code&gt;panic&lt;/code&gt; —&#xA;and dividing by zero is its classic stage.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-emergency-brake&#34;&gt;The emergency brake&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#the-emergency-brake&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Syntax: panic(&amp;lt;value&amp;gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Divide&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        panic(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;division by zero&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;panic&lt;/code&gt; halts normal execution immediately and unwinds the stack, running&#xA;deferred cleanup on the way out. Unrecovered, it crashes the program. That&#xA;sounds violent because it is — which is why panics are reserved for&#xA;&lt;strong&gt;programmer errors&lt;/strong&gt;: broken invariants, impossible states, misuse of an&#xA;API. A zero divisor passed to integer division is exactly that: the caller&#xA;violated the contract, and pretending otherwise would compute garbage with&#xA;a straight face.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Factorial</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/factorial/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/factorial/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;factorial&#34;&gt;Factorial&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#factorial&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Go has exactly one loop keyword: &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt;. No &lt;code&gt;while&lt;/code&gt;, no &lt;code&gt;do&lt;/code&gt;, no &lt;code&gt;foreach&lt;/code&gt;.&#xA;Every repetition in the language — counting, waiting, iterating — is some&#xA;shape of &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt;. This exercise teaches the first shape: the counting loop.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;dissecting-the-header&#34;&gt;Dissecting the header&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#dissecting-the-header&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Syntax: for &amp;lt;init&amp;gt;; &amp;lt;condition&amp;gt;; &amp;lt;post&amp;gt; { &amp;lt;body&amp;gt; }&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Factorial&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;++&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;*=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Three clauses separated by semicolons: declare the counter, state when to&#xA;keep going, say how to advance. &lt;code&gt;i++&lt;/code&gt; adds one — Go has &lt;code&gt;++&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;--&lt;/code&gt; as&#xA;&lt;em&gt;statements only&lt;/em&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;x = i++&lt;/code&gt; doesn&amp;rsquo;t compile and nobody misses it.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Sum To</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/sum_to/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/sum_to/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;sum-to&#34;&gt;Sum To&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#sum-to&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The previous exercise taught &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt; with all three clauses. Now meet the&#xA;shape Go uses &lt;em&gt;instead of&lt;/em&gt; &lt;code&gt;while&lt;/code&gt; — because there is no &lt;code&gt;while&lt;/code&gt; keyword to&#xA;reach for.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-loop-with-one-clause&#34;&gt;The loop with one clause&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#the-loop-with-one-clause&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Syntax: for &amp;lt;condition&amp;gt; { &amp;lt;body&amp;gt; }&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &amp;gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// runs until n reaches 0&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Drop the init and post clauses and &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt; becomes a condition loop: keep&#xA;going while the condition holds. It&amp;rsquo;s the same keyword doing a different&#xA;job, which means one fewer concept to learn and one fewer keyword to&#xA;misremember at 2 AM.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Sum Range</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/sum_range/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/sum_range/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;sum-range&#34;&gt;Sum Range&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#sum-range&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Counting loops count. But most real loops walk through &lt;em&gt;things&lt;/em&gt; — and for&#xA;that, Go gives &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt; a second companion: &lt;code&gt;range&lt;/code&gt;. This exercise also smuggles&#xA;in your first collection type. Two lessons, one stub.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;your-first-slice&#34;&gt;Your first slice&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#your-first-slice&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;[]&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;{&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;} &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// a slice of ints: an ordered, growable sequence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;slice&lt;/strong&gt; is Go&amp;rsquo;s workhorse list: &lt;code&gt;[]int&lt;/code&gt; reads &amp;ldquo;slice of int,&amp;rdquo; and the&#xA;literal in braces holds the elements. Slices appear everywhere ahead —&#xA;test tables already used them anonymously — so get comfortable at a glance.&#xA;One property matters today: a &lt;code&gt;nil&lt;/code&gt; slice (the zero value, no elements at&#xA;all) is perfectly valid, and ranging over it simply runs zero times. The&#xA;test&amp;rsquo;s &lt;code&gt;SumRange(nil)&lt;/code&gt; row proves it.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Overflow</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/overflow/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/overflow/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;overflow&#34;&gt;Overflow&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#overflow&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Your calculator has been suspiciously well-behaved: every sum fit, every test&#xA;told the truth. Time to break that streak — on purpose.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The exercise in &lt;code&gt;exercises/02_calculator/08_overflow&lt;/code&gt; asks an uncomfortable&#xA;question: what happens when the answer doesn&amp;rsquo;t fit the type? The full&#xA;integer model could fill a chapter on its own. You&amp;rsquo;ll get the slice that&#xA;matters — spotting the wrap and picking a fix — and leave the rest for the&#xA;day you meet it in the wild.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Saturating Add</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/saturating/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/saturating/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;saturating-add&#34;&gt;Saturating Add&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#saturating-add&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The overflow exercise taught you to &lt;em&gt;report&lt;/em&gt; the wrap. But sometimes the&#xA;caller can&amp;rsquo;t do anything with a flag — a volume knob, a progress bar, a&#xA;pixel brightness has nowhere to put &amp;ldquo;error.&amp;rdquo; For those, there&amp;rsquo;s a third&#xA;answer between wrapping and panicking: &lt;strong&gt;clamp&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;clamping-instead-of-wrapping&#34;&gt;Clamping instead of wrapping&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#clamping-instead-of-wrapping&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Saturating&lt;/strong&gt; arithmetic pins the result at the type&amp;rsquo;s boundary instead of&#xA;wrapping around it:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;SaturatingAdd&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;)  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// 30  — fits, plain addition&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;SaturatingAdd&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// 255 — would wrap to 44, clamped at MaxUint8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;200 + 100 is 300; the honest &lt;code&gt;uint8&lt;/code&gt; answer doesn&amp;rsquo;t exist, so 255 — the&#xA;nearest representable value — stands in. Audio mixers, image filters, and&#xA;game health bars all saturate: better slightly wrong in the right direction&#xA;than catastrophically wrapped.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Conversions</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/conversions/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/conversions/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;conversions&#34;&gt;Conversions&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#conversions&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The integers lesson laid down the law: no implicit conversion, ever. This&#xA;exercise is the other half of that law — the explicit machinery for moving&#xA;values between types, and the two very different fates awaiting them.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-one-syntax&#34;&gt;The one syntax&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#the-one-syntax&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Every numeric conversion in Go looks identical:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Syntax: &amp;lt;Type&amp;gt;(&amp;lt;value&amp;gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;int64(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// x, whatever its old type, viewed as int64&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;No keywords, no cast operators, no &amp;ldquo;convert&amp;rdquo; builtin — the type name &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt;&#xA;the operation. Once you&amp;rsquo;ve seen &lt;code&gt;uint32(multiplier)&lt;/code&gt;, you&amp;rsquo;ve seen them all.&#xA;Uniformity like this is deliberate: conversions should be visually boring,&#xA;because boring code gets reviewed correctly.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Div Mod</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/divmod/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/divmod/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;div-mod&#34;&gt;Div Mod&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#div-mod&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The syntax lesson promised that multiple return values are normal in Go.&#xA;This exercise cashes that promise in: division produces &lt;em&gt;two&lt;/em&gt; answers, and&#xA;Go hands you both at once.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;one-call-two-results&#34;&gt;One call, two results&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#one-call-two-results&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Syntax: return &amp;lt;first&amp;gt;, &amp;lt;second&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;DivMod&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;) (&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;quotient&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;remainder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;) {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The caller unpacks them the same way:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;DivMod&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// q == 2, r == 1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;No output parameters, no tuple type, no result struct for a two-value&#xA;answer. When a computation naturally yields a pair — quotient and&#xA;remainder, value and flag, result and error — the signature says so&#xA;directly. You&amp;rsquo;ve already used this shape three times over: &lt;code&gt;(sum, overflow)&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;(value, ok)&lt;/code&gt;-style checks, and now &lt;code&gt;(quotient, remainder)&lt;/code&gt;.&#xA;Same rhythm, new verse.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Floats</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/floats/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/floats/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;floats&#34;&gt;Floats&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#floats&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Integers had a strict chaperone: no mixing without conversion. Floats look&#xA;friendlier — until you notice the stub&amp;rsquo;s bug hiding in plain sight:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;Half&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;float64&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; float64(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// looks right, halves wrong&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;Half(5)&lt;/code&gt; returns 2, not 2.5. The conversion is correct and the division is&#xA;correct; the &lt;em&gt;order&lt;/em&gt; is the bug. Integer division runs first (&lt;code&gt;5/2 == 2&lt;/code&gt;,&#xA;truncated, remainder abandoned), and converting the corpse to &lt;code&gt;float64&lt;/code&gt;&#xA;can&amp;rsquo;t resurrect the &lt;code&gt;.5&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Runes</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/runes/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/runes/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;runes&#34;&gt;Runes&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#runes&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Count the characters in &lt;code&gt;&amp;quot;héllo&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt;. Five — obviously. Now ask Go:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;len(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;héllo&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// 6?!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Both answers are right; they&amp;rsquo;re answering different questions. &lt;code&gt;len&lt;/code&gt;&#xA;counts &lt;strong&gt;bytes&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;é&lt;/code&gt; needs two bytes in UTF-8. The string holds six&#xA;bytes that render as five characters. Everything in this exercise follows&#xA;from that split.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;bytes-are-storage-runes-are-characters&#34;&gt;Bytes are storage, runes are characters&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#bytes-are-storage-runes-are-characters&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A Go &lt;code&gt;string&lt;/code&gt; is a read-only sequence of bytes. A &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;rune&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; is a Unicode&#xA;code point — what you mean by &amp;ldquo;character&amp;rdquo; — and it&amp;rsquo;s an alias for &lt;code&gt;int32&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Strings</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/strings/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/strings/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;strings&#34;&gt;Strings&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#strings&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Strings support &lt;code&gt;==&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;+&lt;/code&gt;, slice like arrays — and then, just as you&#xA;reach for them, refuse to be changed. Try it:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;hello&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;[&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;] = &lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;H&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// compile error: cannot assign to s[0]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Strings are immutable.&lt;/strong&gt; There is no edit-in-place, no character&#xA;replacement, no append-to-same-string. Every &amp;ldquo;modification&amp;rdquo; builds a brand&#xA;new string from pieces. Once that clicks, the whole standard library&amp;rsquo;s&#xA;shape makes sense.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;surgery-with-slices&#34;&gt;Surgery with slices&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#surgery-with-slices&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Reading a piece is free — slicing works exactly as on arrays:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Bitwise operators</title>
				<link>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/bitwise/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>http://localhost:1313/100-exercises-to-learn-go/02_calculator/bitwise/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;bitwise-operators&#34;&gt;Bitwise operators&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#bitwise-operators&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Fourteen exercises of arithmetic, and numbers have behaved like quantities.&#xA;Now they become &lt;em&gt;switchboards&lt;/em&gt;: individual bits flipped, combined, and&#xA;tested. The calculator section closes with the lowest-level arithmetic Go&#xA;offers — and the strangest-looking constants you&amp;rsquo;ve met.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;bits-as-flags&#34;&gt;Bits as flags&lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#bits-as-flags&#34;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;code&gt;uint8&lt;/code&gt; holds eight independent on/off switches. The operators manipulate&#xA;them directly:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// OR:  a switch is on if it&amp;#39;s on in either side&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// AND: a switch is on only if it&amp;#39;s on in both sides&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; ^ &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// XOR: a switch is on if the sides disagree&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;amp;^&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// AND NOT: switches on in a but off in b (Go&amp;#39;s &amp;#34;clear&amp;#34; operator)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// shift left n places (multiplies by 2ⁿ)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// shift right n places (divides by 2ⁿ)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;amp;^&lt;/code&gt; deserves a stare — most languages spell it &lt;code&gt;&amp;amp; ~&lt;/code&gt;, but Go mints a&#xA;dedicated operator so clearing bits reads as one thought. &lt;code&gt;p &amp;amp;^ Write&lt;/code&gt;&#xA;means &amp;ldquo;permissions minus write,&amp;rdquo; no parentheses required.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
